<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Opava</h1><p class="st-meta">1269 CE – 1918 CE (approx.) · capital Opava</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Duchy carved out of northern Moravia for a son of King Ottokar II, later ruled by the Liechtensteins and capital of Austrian Silesia until 1918.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1269 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottokar-ii-of-bohemia">King Ottokar II of Bohemia</a> gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opava">Opava</a> region of northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Moravia</a> to his illegitimate son Nicholas I. Nicholas's descendants ruled it as a separate duchy, and it gradually came to be counted among the Silesian duchies rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opava">Opava</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslids</a> ruled until the 15th century, after which the duchy passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-podebrady">Poděbrady</a> family, the Hungarian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. In 1614 it was granted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-i-prince-of-liechtenstein">Karl I</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-liechtenstein">Liechtenstein</a>, whose family kept the ducal title. Opava was a town of German and Czech speakers, cloth and trade, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> after 1620.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1742 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> took the northern part; the rest became the core of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-upper-and-lower-silesia">Austrian Silesia</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opava">Opava</a> as capital, and hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-troppau">Congress of Troppau</a> in 1820. In 1918 it joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> and is now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-opava.jpg" alt="Duchy of Opava" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Johannes Rössel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mdina,_Malta.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1269 CE – 1918 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Opava</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nicholas I of Opava, Karl I of Liechtenstein</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Czech</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duchy of Troppau</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1594&amp;focus=duchy-of-opava" aria-label="Duchy of Opava on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5090 440 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Opava lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5410 696L5417 692M5410 696L5383 718M5410 696L5330 692M5410 696L5410 696M5410 696L5405 692"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5417 692h0M5383 718h0M5330 692h0M5410 696h0M5405 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5410 696h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5410 696h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Opava. <a href="/atlas?year=1594&amp;focus=duchy-of-opava">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
